---
import { rootCSS } from '../renderer/tokens.js';
// The piece. One page, scenes as sections. See docs/10-sprint-02.md.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const site = `https://oddurs.github.io${base}/`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <title>A shape that never repeats</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="One ordinary-looking shape can cover an infinite floor, and the pattern can never repeat. Here is why it can't."
    />
    <link rel="canonical" href={site} />

    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="A shape that never repeats" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="One tile covers an endless floor and the pattern never repeats. Slide it, search it, zoom out — and see why it can't."
    />
    <meta property="og:url" content={site} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={`${site}og.png`} />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta
      property="og:image:alt"
      content="A patch of the einstein tiling, coloured by its nested clusters."
    />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <noscript>
      <style>
        .stage { display: none; }
        /* Both promise an interaction that does not exist without JS. */
        .handoff, .cue { display: none; }
        /* Promote every takeaway from a quiet aside to the scene's actual
           prose, since it is now the only thing carrying it. */
        .takeaway {
          padding-left: 0;
          border-left: 0;
          font-size: 1em;
          color: var(--fg);
        }
        .no-js-note { display: block !important; }
      </style>
    </noscript>

    <article>
      <header class="lede" data-scene="hook" data-decorative>
        <figure class="hero" aria-hidden="true">
          <canvas data-canvas></canvas>
        </figure>
        <p class="eyebrow">The einstein tile</p>
        <h1>A shape that never repeats</h1>
        <p class="standfirst">
          In 2022 a retired print technician, cutting shapes out of card at his
          kitchen table, found something mathematicians had hunted for fifty
          years: a single tile that covers an endless floor without the pattern
          ever repeating. Here is what that means, and why it can’t.
        </p>
        <p class="cue" aria-hidden="true"><span>Scroll</span></p>
      </header>

      <!-- Scene 2 — meet the hat -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="hat" data-scroll style="--beats: 4" aria-labelledby="h-hat">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-hat">The shape itself</h2>
          <p>
            Mathematicians had been closing in on this since the 1960s. The first
            set of tiles that could cover a floor without repeating needed 20,426
            different shapes. That came down to six, then to two, by 1974 — and
            then stopped dead for half a century. Nobody could get to one, and
            nobody could prove one was impossible.
          </p>
          <p>
            So it is worth seeing what finally did it, because the answer is
            almost disappointing. Keep scrolling, and watch it get built.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-track" data-track>
          <figure class="stage" data-sticky>
            <canvas
              data-canvas
              role="img"
              aria-label="The hat, built from eight kites cut out of three hexagons."
              aria-describedby="alt-hat"
            ></canvas>
            <figcaption class="hud">
              <p data-caption class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
              <div class="hud-row slider-row">
                <label for="step-2" class="visually-hidden">construction step</label>
                <input id="step-2" data-step type="range" />
                <span data-readout class="meter" aria-live="polite"></span>
              </div>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <ol class="beats">
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              Start with a floor of hexagons. Ordinary, and it repeats.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>Cut each hexagon into six kites.</li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>Now take eight of those kites…</li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              …and that is the whole shape. Nothing exotic: eight kites off a
              hexagon floor.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p id="alt-hat" class="takeaway">
          The hat is eight kites cut from a hexagon floor — a thirteen-sided
          polygon with no unusual angles. Shapes like it had been catalogued and
          overlooked before, because nobody had tried to tile with one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 3 — try to make it repeat -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="repeat" data-hands-on aria-labelledby="h-repeat">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-repeat">What “repeats” actually means</h2>
          <p>
            That shape can cover a floor forever without the pattern repeating.
            To see why that is strange, it helps to pin down what repeating
            actually is: a pattern repeats when you can slide a copy of it onto
            itself and have everything land. Not roughly — exactly. That is the
            whole definition.
          </p>
          <p>
            Try it on a floor of hexagons first. Then try the same thing on the
            hat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="handoff">Your turn</p>

        <figure class="stage">
          <canvas
            data-canvas
            tabindex="0"
            role="img"
            aria-label="A tiling with a movable copy of itself laid over it. Drag to slide the copy; tiles that land exactly on a tile are highlighted."
            aria-describedby="alt-repeat"
          ></canvas>
          <figcaption class="hud">
            <h3 data-title class="hud-title"></h3>
            <p data-prompt class="hud-prompt" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="gauge">
              <div class="track"><div data-bar class="bar"></div></div>
              <span data-meter class="meter" aria-live="polite"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="hud-row">
              <button data-next type="button" hidden></button>
              <button data-nudge type="button">Try another slide</button>
              <button data-reset type="button">Show me the best there is</button>
            </div>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p id="alt-repeat" class="takeaway">
          A hexagon floor slides onto itself perfectly, in many directions. The
          einstein tiling never does, in any. Some tiles always land — but the
          more of the floor you look at, the worse the best slide gets.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 4 — order is not randomness -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="recurrence" data-hands-on aria-labelledby="h-recur">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-recur">But it isn’t random</h2>
          <p>
            At this point there are two reasonable wrong conclusions. One is
            that it nearly repeats — plenty of tiles did land, after all. The
            other is that it is simply a mess, a shape thrown down at random.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both are wrong, and the same demonstration answers them. Point at any
            patch of the tiling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="handoff">Your turn</p>

        <figure class="stage">
          <canvas
            data-canvas
            tabindex="0"
            role="img"
            aria-label="A patch of the tiling. Select a group of tiles to highlight every other place the same group occurs."
            aria-describedby="alt-recur"
          ></canvas>
          <figcaption class="hud">
            <p data-prompt class="hud-prompt" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <div class="hud-row">
              <button data-pick type="button">Pick a patch</button>
              <span data-readout class="meter" aria-live="polite"></span>
            </div>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p id="alt-recur" class="takeaway">
          Any patch you can point at occurs again elsewhere — typically dozens of
          times within view, and infinitely often on an endless floor. The tiling
          is highly ordered. What it lacks is a single slide that carries the
          whole of it onto itself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 5 — the hierarchy -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="hierarchy" data-scroll style="--beats: 5" aria-labelledby="h-hierarchy">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-hierarchy">Why it can’t</h2>
          <p>
            So the tiling is orderly, and it never repeats. Both at once. The
            reason is visible if you stop looking at the tiles and start looking
            at how they are organised. Keep going, and pull back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-track" data-track>
          <figure class="stage" data-sticky>
            <canvas
              data-canvas
              role="img"
              aria-label="The tiling, coloured by which cluster each tile belongs to at the current grouping level."
              aria-describedby="alt-hierarchy"
            ></canvas>
            <figcaption class="hud">
              <p data-caption class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
              <div class="hud-row slider-row">
                <span aria-hidden="true" class="end">tiles</span>
                <label for="stage-5" class="visually-hidden">grouping level</label>
                <input id="stage-5" data-stage type="range" />
                <span aria-hidden="true" class="end">whole</span>
                <span data-readout class="meter" aria-live="polite"></span>
              </div>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <ol class="beats">
            <li class="beat" data-beat>One shape, laid down over and over.</li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              The tiles clump into groups — always the same four kinds.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              Those groups clump into bigger groups. The same four kinds again.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              And again, larger still. Every level looks like the one below it.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              It never stops — so there is no block you could stamp out, at any
              size. That is why it can never repeat.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p id="alt-hierarchy" class="takeaway">
          Tiles group into four kinds of cluster; those clusters group into the
          same four kinds, larger; and so on without end. A repeating pattern
          needs a block you can stamp out. This has no such block at any size —
          which is exactly why it can never repeat.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 7 — the continuum -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="continuum" data-scroll style="--beats: 5" aria-labelledby="h-continuum">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-continuum">And it isn’t even one shape</h2>
          <p>
            Here is the part that still seems unreasonable. The hat is not a
            special polygon that happens to work. It is one point on a
            continuous family of shapes — and the whole family works, the same
            way, for the same reason.
          </p>
          <p>Keep going, and watch the tile stretch.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-track" data-track>
          <figure class="stage" data-sticky>
            <canvas
              data-canvas
              role="img"
              aria-label="A tiling whose tile stretches continuously from the hat to a longer shape called the turtle, without the arrangement changing."
              aria-describedby="alt-continuum"
            ></canvas>
            <figcaption class="hud">
              <p data-caption class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
              <div class="hud-row slider-row">
                <span aria-hidden="true" class="end">hat</span>
                <label for="morph" class="visually-hidden">shape</label>
                <input id="morph" data-morph type="range" />
                <span aria-hidden="true" class="end">turtle</span>
                <span data-readout class="meter" aria-live="polite"></span>
              </div>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <!-- The beats are the scene's captions, authored once, here. The
               scene reads them back for its live region, so a screen reader and
               a scrolling reader are never told different things. -->
          <ol class="beats">
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              The hat. Every edge is about to stretch — but the arrangement will
              not move.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              Every edge is stretching or shrinking, and nothing has come apart.
              No gaps, no overlaps.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              Halfway. A shape with no name, and it tiles exactly like the
              others.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              Still going. Still the same tiling underneath.
            </li>
            <li class="beat" data-beat>
              The turtle. A different shape, in exactly the same arrangement.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p id="alt-continuum" class="takeaway">
          Every edge of the hat has one of two lengths. Change those two lengths
          and you get a different shape — which tiles in exactly the same
          arrangement, and just as unrepeatingly. The hat and the turtle are two
          points on that family; there are infinitely many in between, and
          almost all of them are einsteins too.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p class="no-js-note">
        The figures on this page are interactive and need JavaScript. Without it
        you get the argument in words, which is most of it.
      </p>

      <footer class="outro">
        <h2>Afterwards</h2>
        <p>
          The shape is called <em>the hat</em>. David Smith found it in
          November 2022, by hand, with card and scissors; he worked out what it
          meant with Joseph Samuel Myers, Craig S. Kaplan and Chaim
          Goodman-Strauss, and they published the proof four months later.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is one asterisk, and it is worth knowing. Every tiling by the hat
          uses the shape together with its mirror image — about one tile in eight
          is flipped. Some people felt that made it two tiles rather than one. Two
          months later the same four authors settled the argument with a second
          shape, the <em>spectre</em>, which needs no mirrors at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          What nobody has is a reason. There is no theory that says which shapes
          will behave like this, which is why the hat sat in catalogues for years
          with nobody noticing — it does not look like anything in particular. It
          still doesn’t.
        </p>
        <p class="make-link">
          <a href={`${base}/make/`}>Make one of your own →</a>
        </p>
        <p class="fine">
          <span>Built from the authors’ own substitution data.</span>{' '}
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10798">Read the paper</a>{' · '}
          <a href="https://github.com/oddurs/einstein-tile">source</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </article>

    <script>
      import { mountScenes } from '../scenes/stage.js';
      import { mountHookScene } from '../scenes/hook.js';
      import { mountHatScene } from '../scenes/thehat.js';
      import { mountRepeatScene } from '../scenes/repeat.js';
      import { mountRecurrenceScene } from '../scenes/recurrence.js';
      import { mountContinuumScene } from '../scenes/continuum.js';
      import { mountHierarchyScene } from '../scenes/hierarchy.js';
      import { scrollDriven } from '../scenes/scroll.js';

      mountScenes({
        hook: mountHookScene,
        // Four discrete steps, so progress snaps to them — a figure resting
        // between two stages reads as a bug rather than as a step.
        hat: scrollDriven(mountHatScene, '[data-step]', 4),
        // `repeat` and `recurrence` are deliberately absent from the
        // scroll-driven list. They are about the reader's own hands — repeat
        // was rebuilt precisely because someone said they didn't get it, and
        // the fix was to make you slide the copy yourself. Driving those from
        // scroll would hand back the watching and take away the doing.
        repeat: mountRepeatScene,
        recurrence: mountRecurrenceScene,
        // Five discrete stages. The earlier claim that this stalled 185ms was
        // a bad measurement — the test fed fractional values into a step=1
        // control, so every call minted a new colour token and missed the
        // renderer's path cache. On integer stages it costs 8.3ms like the rest.
        hierarchy: scrollDriven(mountHierarchyScene, '[data-stage]', 5),
        continuum: scrollDriven(mountContinuumScene, '[data-morph]'),
      });

      // The cue has one job and stops the moment it is done. Asking twice is
      // nagging, and a reader who has scrolled has already answered.
      const cue = document.querySelector('.cue');
      if (cue) {
        const answered = () => {
          if (window.scrollY < window.innerHeight * 0.35) return;
          cue.classList.add('is-answered');
          removeEventListener('scroll', answered);
        };
        addEventListener('scroll', answered, { passive: true });
      }
    </script>

    <style is:global set:html={rootCSS(base)}></style>

    <style is:global>

      * { box-sizing: border-box; }

      html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
        * { transition: none !important; animation: none !important; }
      }

      body {
        margin: 0;
        /* The hook bleeds past the article to reach the page edges, which
           otherwise adds up to ~60px of horizontal scroll at every width below
           desktop.

           `clip`, not `hidden`. They look identical and are not: `hidden` makes
           this element a scroll container, and a scroll container is what
           `position: sticky` sticks to — so every sticky stage would pin to the
           document rather than to the viewport and simply never move. `clip`
           does the clipping without the scrollport, which is exactly what was
           wanted here in the first place. */
        overflow-x: clip;
        background: var(--bg);
        color: var(--fg);
        font-family: var(--face);
        /* Match the fallback's x-height to the webfont's so the swap does not
           reflow the page under the reader. */
        font-size-adjust: var(--face-adjust);
        font-size: var(--type-body);
        line-height: var(--leading-body);
        /* Old-style figures in running prose: they have ascenders and
           descenders, so they sit *in* the line rather than shouting from it.
           Georgia happens to default to these — stating it means the choice
           survives a change of typeface. */
        font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums proportional-nums;
        /* Spend the shrink TeX would have spent. See `FACE_WORD_SPACING`. */
        word-spacing: var(--face-word-spacing);
        font-kerning: normal;
        font-variant-ligatures: common-ligatures contextual;
        /* Break paragraphs looking further ahead than the next line. The
           browser is still greedy, but less so; TeX's whole-paragraph
           optimisation is what this approximates. */
        text-wrap: pretty;
        -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;
      }

      article {
        max-width: 62rem;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 0 max(20px, env(safe-area-inset-left)) 8rem;
      }

      .prose, .lede, .outro, .takeaway {
        max-width: var(--measure);
        margin-inline: auto;
      }
      /* Short and set larger, so it can run wider without tiring the eye. */
      .standfirst { max-width: var(--measure-lead); }

      .lede {
        position: relative;
        /* Clips the hook's bleed at its source. `overflow-x: hidden` on <body>
           alone does not work here — the scrollport is <html>, so the overflow
           propagates past it and you still get a scrollbar. Clipping the
           containing block is the fix that actually holds. */
        overflow: hidden;
        padding: 20vh 0 12vh;
      }

      /* The hook is decoration behind the title: aria-hidden, pointer-events
         none so it never eats a scroll, and faded so the type stays the thing
         you read first. */
      .hero {
        position: absolute;
        inset: -10vh -22vw auto;
        margin: 0;
        height: 52vh;
        pointer-events: none;
        opacity: 0.26;
        /* Two masks, multiplied: a radial one to keep it from meeting the page
           edges, and a linear one so it has faded out entirely before the
           standfirst begins. Texture behind body copy is the fastest way to
           make an editorial page look cheap and read badly. */
        /* Declared plainly first, then enhanced. A single mask works
           everywhere; the composited pair is nicer where it is supported. If
           mask-composite silently fails the hook would render at full strength
           over the standfirst, which is a cheap-looking failure rather than an
           obviously broken one — so the simple version has to stand alone. */
        -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(#000 42%, transparent 72%);
        mask-image: linear-gradient(#000 42%, transparent 72%);
      }
      @supports (mask-composite: intersect) {
        .hero {
          mask-image:
            radial-gradient(110% 80% at 50% 25%, #000 25%, transparent 75%),
            linear-gradient(#000 42%, transparent 72%);
          mask-composite: intersect;
        }
      }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        .hero { opacity: 0.4; }
      }
      .hero canvas { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; }

      .lede > :not(.hero) { position: relative; }

      .eyebrow {
        margin: 0 0 1.2rem;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: var(--type-fine);
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: 0.09em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      h1 {
        margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
        font-size: var(--type-display);
        font-weight: 400;
        line-height: var(--leading-display);
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
        text-wrap: balance;
        font-variant-ligatures: common-ligatures discretionary-ligatures;
      }

      .standfirst {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: var(--type-lead);
        line-height: var(--leading-lead);
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      h2 {
        margin: 0 0 0.8rem;
        font-size: var(--type-section);
        font-weight: 400;
        line-height: var(--leading-section);
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        text-wrap: balance;
      }

      p { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }

      .scene { margin: 0 0 14vh; }

      /* The stage is deliberately NOT full-height. The canvas takes
         `touch-action: none` so it can own drag and pinch, which means a
         full-bleed canvas would swallow vertical scrolling and strand a reader
         on a phone. Keeping it under two-thirds of the viewport guarantees
         there is always ordinary page above and below to scroll with. */
      .stage {
        position: relative;
        margin: 2rem 0;
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        border-radius: 16px;
        overflow: hidden;
        background: var(--bg);
      }

      .stage canvas:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: -3px;
      }

      .stage canvas {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 62vh; /* fallback for browsers without dvh */
        height: 62dvh;
        max-height: 640px;
        touch-action: none;
      }

      /* ---- Scroll-driven scenes -------------------------------------------
         The figure pins while the section scrolls past it, and the beats — the
         sentences it is evidence for — rise through the space underneath. This
         is the fix for the thing that was actually wrong: at every width, a
         stage took ~78% of the viewport, so the figure and the sentence
         explaining it were never on screen at the same time.

         The height cap can be lifted here, and only here, because these
         canvases pass `gestures: false` and so take `touch-action: pan-y`. A
         vertical swipe on them always scrolls the page. There is no trap
         available to build. */
      [data-scroll] .scroll-track {
        position: relative;
        /* The pinned height. Everything else is sized against it, so the parts
           cannot drift apart. */
        --pin: 50dvh;
        /* Scroll spent on each beat — the one number that tunes how long the
           piece feels, and it was needed immediately. At 62dvh the piece came
           out at 17.1 viewports against a planned 13, so the plan's estimate
           was 30% optimistic and this is what paid for it. Nothing else moved,
           which was the argument for keeping the knob in one place. */
        --beat-travel: 45dvh;
        min-height: calc(var(--pin) + 11rem + var(--beats, 4) * var(--beat-travel));
      }

      [data-scroll] .stage {
        position: sticky;
        top: max(1rem, 3dvh);
        /* Above the beats, which slide behind its lower edge. */
        z-index: 1;
        margin-top: 0;
      }

      [data-scroll] .stage canvas {
        height: var(--pin);
        max-height: 560px;
      }

      /* The HUD keeps the slider, which is doing three jobs at once: the
         keyboard path, the reduced-motion interface, and a progress indicator
         that happens to move as you scroll. It loses the drag hint, because
         the beats now say what it used to. */
      [data-scroll] .hud {
        padding: 10px 18px;
      }

      /* The readout named the shape beside an end label that already named it
         — "turtle · the turtle" — and the beats now say it in a sentence. Keep
         it for the live region, drop it from the layout. */
      [data-scroll] .slider-row .meter {
        position: absolute;
        width: 1px;
        height: 1px;
        overflow: hidden;
        clip-path: inset(50%);
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      /* The beats pin too, just under the figure, and cross-fade.
         They used to scroll past it, which was wrong for a reason worth
         recording: a beat travelled one window-height during its turn, but had
         to cross the *whole* pinned figure to get out of sight — so it spent
         part of its turn behind the figure, leaving an orphaned last line
         floating under the card. Pinning removes the geometry problem instead
         of tuning it, and holds the sentence still while its figure moves. */
      [data-scroll] .beats {
        position: sticky;
        top: calc(var(--pin) + 7.5rem);
        /* Every beat in one grid cell, so they cross-fade in place and the
           block never changes height as the text changes. */
        display: grid;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 0;
        list-style: none;
        min-height: 7rem;
        max-width: var(--measure-caption);
      }

      [data-scroll] .beat {
        grid-area: 1 / 1;
        font-size: var(--type-body);
        line-height: var(--leading-body);
        color: var(--fg);
        opacity: 0;
        transition: opacity 240ms ease;
      }

      [data-scroll] .beat.is-current { opacity: 1; }

      /* ---- Desktop: the beats beside the figure ----------------------------
         Sprint 10 built the mechanism once, for a narrow column, and then used
         it at every width. Measured, that meant the continuum figure filled
         **35% of its canvas width** at 1440 and the hat 42%: the canvas was
         950×450 — 2.1:1 — and the tiling it draws is square, so two thirds of
         the box was background. The phone was fine at 74–88%, because the phone
         is what the stacked layout was designed for.

         Side by side fixes both halves at once. The figure gets a box shaped
         like the thing inside it, and the beats stop being something you scroll
         *to* and become something you read *beside* it.

         The breakpoint is 900px because that is where two columns still leave
         the beats a comfortable measure — below it, stacked is genuinely
         better, which is why the phone layout is untouched. */
      @media (min-width: 900px) {
        [data-scroll] .scroll-track {
          /* Bleed into the gutters the article was leaving unused: 224px a side
             at 1440, 464px at 1920. A figure is not prose and has no measure to
             respect. Done with margins rather than a transform, because a
             transformed ancestor is a classic way to break `position: sticky`. */
          --bleed: min(1180px, calc(100vw - 5rem));
          width: var(--bleed);
          margin-inline-start: calc((100% - var(--bleed)) / 2);

          /* The figure is now tall enough to be worth pinning higher. */
          --pin: 72dvh;

          display: grid;
          grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.45fr) minmax(17rem, 0.8fr);
          /* One row that fills the track, so both sticky children have a tall
             containing block to travel inside. */
          grid-template-rows: 1fr;
          column-gap: 2.75rem;
          align-items: start;
        }

        [data-scroll] .stage {
          grid-area: 1 / 1;
        }

        [data-scroll] .stage canvas {
          max-height: 760px;
        }

        /* On a phone the beats cross-fade in one place, because there is only
           room for one. A desktop column has room for all of them, so it shows
           all of them: the current one lit, the rest dimmed but legible.

           This is worth more than the space it fills. A reader can see how many
           beats the scene has, which one they are on, and what is coming — so
           the scene has a visible shape instead of being an unknown length of
           scrolling. It is also, for free, most of what a progress indicator
           was going to be. */
        [data-scroll] .beats {
          grid-area: 1 / 2;
          display: flex;
          flex-direction: column;
          gap: 1.1rem;
          /* Roughly centred against a 72dvh figure pinned at 3dvh. */
          top: calc(3dvh + 7rem);
          min-height: 0;
          max-width: none;
        }

        [data-scroll] .beat {
          opacity: 0.26;
          transition: opacity 240ms ease;
        }

        [data-scroll] .beat.is-current { opacity: 1; }
      }

      /* Nothing above is allowed to depend on motion. Without it the figure is
         an ordinary stage with an ordinary slider, and the beats are an
         ordinary list of sentences — the argument arrives intact, just still. */
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        [data-scroll] .scroll-track { min-height: 0; }
        [data-scroll] .stage,
        [data-scroll] .beats { position: static; }
        [data-scroll] .beats { display: block; }
        [data-scroll] .beat {
          margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
          opacity: 1;
        }
      }

      /* The piece is 14.8 viewports and asks for sustained scrolling almost
         immediately. The hook is the only place a reader decides whether to
         go, and it was making no case at all — so it asks, once, quietly, and
         then gets out of the way the moment the reader has answered. */
      .cue {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.7rem;
        margin: 2.6rem 0 0;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: var(--type-fine);
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: 0.09em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--muted);
        transition: opacity 400ms ease;
      }

      /* A short rule that slides down and back, the length of one polite
         suggestion. */
      .cue::after {
        content: '';
        width: 2.5rem;
        height: 1px;
        background: currentColor;
        transform-origin: left;
        animation: cue-slide 2.4s ease-in-out infinite;
      }

      @keyframes cue-slide {
        0%, 100% { transform: scaleX(0.4); opacity: 0.5; }
        50% { transform: scaleX(1); opacity: 1; }
      }

      .cue.is-answered { opacity: 0; }

      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .cue::after { animation: none; }
      }

      /* ---- The seam between the two modes ---------------------------------
         Three scenes now pin their figure for ~3 viewports and take no touch
         at all. Then `repeat` arrives, where the figure scrolls away like an
         ordinary image and expects to be dragged. Nothing announced that, and
         **a change of mode with no signal reads as a bug** — especially
         landing straight after three scenes that taught the opposite rules.

         So the hands-on scenes get an arrival: a marked handoff, and a stage
         that looks like an object rather than a figure. Not a modal, not a
         gate — a reader who keeps scrolling past without touching anything
         still gets the whole argument, exactly as before. */
      .handoff {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.85rem;
        margin: 2.4rem 0 0;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: var(--type-fine);
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: 0.09em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--accent);
      }

      /* A rule running off to the right: the piece stops and hands over. */
      .handoff::after {
        content: '';
        flex: 1;
        height: 1px;
        background: linear-gradient(
          to right,
          color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 45%, transparent),
          transparent
        );
      }

      /* The stage stops being a figure you look at and becomes a thing you
         touch: it sits on its own surface rather than the page's. */
      [data-hands-on] .stage {
        margin-top: 1rem;
        border-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 26%, var(--line));
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 4%, var(--bg));
      }

      /* Landscape phones: a 62dvh canvas plus a HUD is taller than the whole
         viewport, which traps the reader — the canvas owns touch, so with no
         prose visible there is nothing left to scroll with. Landscape is also
         wide, so put the HUD beside the canvas instead of under it. */
      @media (max-height: 560px) and (min-width: 640px) {
        .stage {
          display: grid;
          grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(16rem, 21rem);
        }
        .stage canvas { height: 54dvh; }
        .hud {
          border-top: none;
          border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
          overflow-y: auto;
        }
      }
      /* Narrow *and* short (a small phone on its side): there is no room for a
         side panel either, so simply give the canvas less. */
      @media (max-height: 560px) and (max-width: 639px) {
        .stage canvas { height: 40dvh; }
        .hud-prompt { min-height: 0; }
      }

      .hud {
        padding: 16px 18px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
        background: var(--panel);
      }

      .hud-title {
        margin: 0 0 6px;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12px;
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      .hud-prompt {
        max-width: var(--measure-caption);
        margin: 0 0 12px;
        font-size: var(--type-body);
        line-height: var(--leading-lead);
        text-wrap: balance;
        min-height: 2.8em;
      }

      .hud-row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; }
      .slider-row { gap: 12px; }

      .gauge {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 12px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
      }

      .track {
        flex: 1;
        height: 8px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.22); /* fallback */
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 12%, transparent);
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      .bar {
        height: 100%;
        width: 0;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: var(--accent);
        transition: width 90ms linear;
      }
      .bar[data-perfect='true'] { background: var(--good); }

      .meter {
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: var(--type-fine);
        /* Lining and tabular: these values change in place, and old-style or
           proportional figures make them jump as the digits change. */
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
        color: var(--muted);
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      .hint {
        display: flex;
        gap: 10px;
        margin: 10px 0 0;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12.5px;
        color: var(--muted);
      }
      .hint .meter { margin-left: auto; }

      .end {
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12px;
        text-transform: lowercase;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      input[type='range'] { flex: 1; min-width: 0; min-height: 44px; }

      button {
        min-height: 44px;
        padding: 0 16px;
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        border-radius: 9px;
        background: transparent;
        color: inherit;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 14px;
      }
      button[data-next]:not([hidden]) {
        border-color: transparent;
        background: var(--fg);
        color: var(--bg);
      }

      :focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
        border-radius: 4px;
      }

      /* The takeaway is the scene's meaning in words. It carries the point for
         anyone who cannot use the canvas, and for anyone who skims. */
      .takeaway {
        margin: 0;
        padding-left: 1rem;
        border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
        /* Body size, not small. This paragraph carries the scene's whole
           argument and is the only thing a reader without JavaScript gets —
           de-emphasise it with colour and the rule, never by shrinking it. */
        font-size: var(--type-body);
        line-height: var(--leading-body);
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      .no-js-note {
        display: none;
        max-width: var(--measure);
        margin: 0 auto 2rem;
        padding-left: 1rem;
        border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
        font-size: 0.9em;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      .make-link {
        margin: 1.6rem 0;
        font-size: 1.05em;
      }
      .make-link a { text-decoration-thickness: 1px; }

      .outro { padding-top: 2rem; }
      .fine { font-size: var(--type-small); color: var(--muted); }
      a { color: inherit; text-underline-offset: 3px; }

      .visually-hidden {
        position: absolute;
        width: 1px;
        height: 1px;
        margin: -1px;
        overflow: hidden;
        clip-path: inset(50%);
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      /* When a figure cannot be shown — no JavaScript, a scene that threw, a
         canvas context that never arrived — drop the empty frame and let the
         takeaway carry the scene instead. The takeaways were written for screen
         readers and skim-readers; they turn out to be the whole no-JS story
         too, which is the nice thing about writing the meaning down once. */
      [data-failed='true'] .stage { display: none; }
      [data-failed='true'] .takeaway {
        padding-left: 0;
        border-left: 0;
        font-size: 1em;
        color: var(--fg);
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
